\f0\b 1 \'b7 Cinematic genealogy
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pulp Fiction
\f0\i0  (1994, Quentin Tarantino, USA)
\f2\b0  \'97 As a foundational post-1990 braided-narrative crime film, it is the structural ancestor whose interlocking, nonlinear storylines 
\f3\i Amores Perros
\f2\i0  inherits, only relocated from L.A. pulp-noir to Mexico City and knotted together by a car crash rather than a briefcase.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Short Cuts
\f0\i0  (1993, Robert Altman, USA)
\f2\b0  \'97 It is the modern template for the mosaic-of-strangers form 
\f3\i Amores Perros
\f2\i0  uses, weaving unrelated city lives into a single fabric, here across Altman's Los Angeles and drawn from Raymond Carver rather than from one collision.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Magnolia
\f0\i0  (1999, Paul Thomas Anderson, USA)
\f2\b0  \'97 It belongs to the same network-narrative lineage of converging fates within a single metropolis, but lets chance and coincidence, rather than one violent accident, be the force that binds its strangers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2 \'b7 Same director, varied (I\'f1\'e1rritu)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 21 Grams
\f0\i0  (2003, Alejandro Gonz\'e1lez I\'f1\'e1rritu, USA)
\f2\b0  \'97 It carries I\'f1\'e1rritu's signature of fate-colliding strangers, but shatters the timeline into nonlinear fragments and transplants the structure from Mexico City to the United States.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Babel
\f0\i0  (2006, Alejandro Gonz\'e1lez I\'f1\'e1rritu, multiple countries)
\f2\b0  \'97 It extends his same interwoven-fates architecture to a global scale, linking a single gunshot across Morocco, Mexico, Japan, and the U.S. instead of one Mexico City intersection.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Birdman
\f0\i0  (2014, Alejandro Gonz\'e1lez I\'f1\'e1rritu, USA)
\f2\b0  \'97 It keeps his raw existential intensity while inverting the form completely, replacing fractured multi-strand editing with the illusion of a single unbroken take inside a Broadway theater.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3 \'b7 Same space-time (Mexico), different gaze
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Y Tu Mam\'e1 Tambi\'e9n
\f0\i0  (2001, Alfonso Cuar\'f3n, Mexico)
\f2\b0  \'97 It unfolds in the same contemporary Mexico and along the same class fault lines, but trades urban brutality for a sun-soaked coming-of-age road movie that drifts out of the capital toward the coast.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 La Zona
\f0\i0  (2007, Rodrigo Pl\'e1, Mexico)
\f2\b0  \'97 It shares the same contemporary Mexico City split by class, but views it from inside a walled wealthy enclave, turning that divide into a tense moral thriller rather than street-level tragedy.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\i\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 G\'fceros
\f0\i0  (2014, Alonso Ruizpalacios, Mexico)
\f2\b0  \'97 It occupies the same Mexico City and its restless youth, yet looks through a playful black-and-white, French-New-Wave lens instead of kinetic realism.\
